KarTrak ACI (Automatic Car Identification) — format provenance and usability
==========================================================================

This file records where the KarTrak ACI symbology comes from, what
governs it, and the basis on which this project implements and
redistributes it.

It is an ENGINEERING INVENTORY, NOT LEGAL ADVICE. Items marked [TO VERIFY]
have not been independently confirmed by the author.


ORIGIN
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Developed by GTE Sylvania (David Jarrett Collins) in the early-to-mid
1960s and adopted by the Association of American Railroads (AAR) Board of
Directors in September 1967 as a mandatory industry standard for
interchange railcars in North America. Field-tested against competing
systems from ABEX and Westinghouse Air Brake Company (Wabco) on the
Pennsylvania Railroad before selection. Withdrawn as a requirement by AAR
ballot in early 1978, following a November 1977 AAR white paper on its
reliability problems, after roughly a decade of mandated use. GTE Sylvania
exited the field in 1972, selling the system to Servo Corporation of
America.

No specific AAR standard-number citation for the 1967 KarTrak/ACI mandate
itself was located. "AAR S-918" is a distinct, later, unrelated standard —
it defines the 128-bit data format for AEI (Automatic Equipment
Identification), the RFID system that replaced KarTrak in the 1990s — and
must not be cited as KarTrak's designation. [TO VERIFY]


SPECIFICATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

No surviving, purchasable AAR standards document for KarTrak/ACI was
located; the system has been defunct since 1978. The colour-to-value
encoding table and label structure implemented here were cross-verified
against two independent technical descriptions that agree exactly on
every table entry:
  - Wikipedia's "KarTrak" article, "Values of label stripes" table (raw
    wikitext, not the rendered/summarized page — see IMPLEMENTATION BASIS)
  - Ian Cranstone, "A guide to ACI (Automatic Car Identification)/Kar Trak
    labels," nakina.net, 2011
A third source (an archived icrr.net page, cited by Wikipedia) independently
corroborates the same 13-line label structure and mod-11 weighted checksum
formula. The worked example decoded from a real photographed label cited
by Wikipedia ("Start 8350199918 Stop 5") was independently reproduced by
this project's own encoder/checksum implementation from only the payload
digits, which is strong evidence the table and checksum were transcribed
correctly rather than merely self-consistent.

Specification TEXT (the cited articles) is copyrighted by its authors. The
SYMBOLOGY it describes — a defunct, AAR-mandated industry scheme from
1967 — is not a work of authorship. This project implements the
symbology from its own original code, and does not reproduce, redistribute
or excerpt the cited articles.


PATENT STATUS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

The foundational optical-scanning patent generally cited for the KarTrak
system, Sylvania's Francis H. Stites and Raymond Alexander, "Mark
Sensing," US 3,225,177 (granted 21 December 1965), expired around 1982
under the pre-1995 17-years-from-grant patent term. Later KarTrak-adjacent
patents identified — Abex's coded-identification patent US 3,366,952
(1966/1968) and several Servo Corporation of America refinements from
1976-1982 (US 3,935,432, US 4,002,889, US 4,329,573) — are similarly all
decades past expiry under the same rule. No evidence of any live patent
covering the symbology itself (as opposed to specific scanner hardware
implementations, all likewise expired) was found. [TO VERIFY]


TRADEMARK
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

No live trademark restriction on implementing or describing KarTrak/ACI is
known to the author. [TO VERIFY] That is a statement about the author's
knowledge, not a finding that no mark subsists: no trademark search was
carried out. "KarTrak" was Sylvania/Servo Corporation branding for a
system that has been defunct and unused since 1978.

A trademark does not restrict implementing a symbology. It restricts
branding — how a product names and presents itself. This project's
`kartrak` format id and documentation use the name descriptively, to
identify the historical symbology being implemented, not as a product
brand.


IMPLEMENTATION BASIS IN THIS PROJECT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

KarTrak ACI is implemented here from the colour-to-value table and label
structure described above, which are facts about a defunct industry
scheme rather than works of authorship. The table, the 13-line label
layout (START, 10 data digits, STOP, mod-11 check digit), and the
mod-11-weighted checksum formula are original Sythos data and code,
verified directly against the raw wikitext of the Wikipedia article (not
the rendered page, after an earlier automated fetch of the same page was
found to have transposed the table's rows and columns) and cross-checked
pixel-for-pixel against that article's own reference SVG diagrams
("Diagram Of a KarTrak ACI Plate.svg", "KarTrak ACI codes.svg") to confirm
the exact geometry of the START/STOP marker glyphs, which are not simple
uniform two-stripe digits. No source code, image asset or constant table
from any other barcode implementation is copied or shipped; this project's
`src/ts/color/` colour-classification primitives (`PolychromeMatrix`,
`toColorImageData`, `classifyGrid`) and the `src/ts/kartrak/` format module
built on them are original Sythos work.


CONCLUSION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

On the basis of the references below, KarTrak ACI is understood to be
freely implementable and redistributable: it is a defunct, once-mandatory
industry scheme from a body (AAR) that itself withdrew it in 1978, its
foundational patents are all decades expired, and no live specification
gatekeeper or purchase requirement (unlike PostBar's unpublished Canada
Post specification, see `licenses/postbar.license` if implemented) stands
between an implementer and the encoding rules. This project's
implementation of it is distributed under the MIT License with no
additional restriction.

References relied upon:
  - Wikipedia, "KarTrak" article, raw wikitext (colour table, label
    structure, checksum formula, worked example)
  - Ian Cranstone, "A guide to ACI (Automatic Car Identification)/Kar Trak
    labels," nakina.net, 2011 (independent colour-table cross-check)
  - "Diagram Of a KarTrak ACI Plate.svg" and "KarTrak ACI codes.svg",
    Wikimedia Commons (geometric verification of digit and START/STOP
    glyphs)
  - US Patent 3,225,177 (Sylvania, "Mark Sensing," granted 1965, expired
    ~1982) and related KarTrak-adjacent patents, all expired [TO VERIFY]

